---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
---

<Layout title="How the Catalyst Signal Works | Earnings Beats & Analyst Upgrades">
  <section class="py-16 bg-[#161B24]">
    <div class="max-w-5xl mx-auto px-4">
      <h1 class="text-4xl font-bold text-center mb-4">How the Catalyst Signal Works</h1>
      <p class="text-xl text-[#CBD5E1] text-center mb-12 max-w-2xl mx-auto">
        A read on stocks that just had a positive catalyst — recent earnings beats and analyst-upgrade momentum.
      </p>

      <!-- Honest framing up front -->
      <div class="bg-gradient-to-r from-amber-900/30 to-amber-800/20 rounded-2xl p-6 mb-12 border border-amber-500/40">
        <div class="flex items-center gap-3 mb-3">
          <span class="text-3xl">🧪</span>
          <h2 class="text-xl font-bold text-amber-300">Experimental — we're tracking this live</h2>
        </div>
        <p class="text-[#CBD5E1]">
          The Catalyst Signal is <strong class="text-white">promising but not proven.</strong> It comes from a
          backtest that pointed the right way, but with only about nine independent years behind it —
          enough to rule things out, not enough to call it settled. So we pre-registered the exact
          formula (frozen, in writing, before launch) and we're now tracking how it does going forward in real
          time. That live track record — not the backtest — is the honest test. Treat it as one more input, not
          a buy signal.
        </p>
      </div>

      <!-- What it measures -->
      <div class="bg-[#080A0F] rounded-2xl p-8 mb-12">
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold mb-2">What it measures</h2>
        <p class="text-[#CBD5E1] mb-6">
          Big winners usually share a <em>change</em> a year earlier — something got better and the market started
          to notice. Static "is it cheap / profitable / small" measures don't predict which stocks run (we checked
          — that's mostly just volatility). What does show up is <strong class="text-white">expectations
          shifting</strong>: companies beating earnings and getting upgraded. The signal tracks four things:
        </p>

        <div class="grid md:grid-cols-2 gap-6">
          <div class="bg-[#161B24] rounded-xl p-6 border border-[rgba(255,255,255,0.06)]">
            <div class="flex items-center gap-3 mb-3">
              <span class="text-2xl">🎯</span>
              <h3 class="font-semibold">Recent earnings surprise</h3>
            </div>
            <p class="text-[#CBD5E1] text-sm">
              How much the company beat (or missed) the analyst EPS estimate at its most recent report. Beats tend
              to drift higher for months afterward — a long-documented pattern (post-earnings-announcement drift).
            </p>
          </div>

          <div class="bg-[#161B24] rounded-xl p-6 border border-[rgba(255,255,255,0.06)]">
            <div class="flex items-center gap-3 mb-3">
              <span class="text-2xl">🔥</span>
              <h3 class="font-semibold">Beat streak</h3>
            </div>
            <p class="text-[#CBD5E1] text-sm">
              How many quarters in a row the company has beaten estimates. A repeated pattern of beats says more
              than a single lucky quarter.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div class="bg-[#161B24] rounded-xl p-6 border border-[rgba(255,255,255,0.06)]">
            <div class="flex items-center gap-3 mb-3">
              <span class="text-2xl">📈</span>
              <h3 class="font-semibold">Net analyst upgrades</h3>
            </div>
            <p class="text-[#CBD5E1] text-sm">
              Upgrades minus downgrades from Wall Street analysts over the trailing 180 days. Rising conviction
              among the people who cover the stock.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div class="bg-[#161B24] rounded-xl p-6 border border-[rgba(255,255,255,0.06)]">
            <div class="flex items-center gap-3 mb-3">
              <span class="text-2xl">⚖️</span>
              <h3 class="font-semibold">Upgrade intensity</h3>
            </div>
            <p class="text-[#CBD5E1] text-sm">
              Those net upgrades as a share of all rating changes, so a stock with light coverage isn't unfairly
              outranked by one with dozens of analysts.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <!-- The score and the tag -->
      <div class="bg-[#080A0F] rounded-2xl p-8 mb-12">
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold mb-4">The Score and the Tag</h2>
        <div class="grid md:grid-cols-2 gap-6">
          <div class="bg-[#161B24] rounded-xl p-6 border border-[#22D3EE33]">
            <h3 class="font-semibold text-[#22D3EE] mb-2">Catalyst Score (0–100)</h3>
            <p class="text-[#CBD5E1] text-sm">
              Each stock is ranked against the rest of the market on the four measures above, and the score is the
              average of those ranks. Higher means a stronger current catalyst signature. If a stock has no
              earnings or analyst coverage at all, we show <strong class="text-white">"No coverage"</strong> — not
              a zero, because absence of data isn't a bad signal.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="bg-[#161B24] rounded-xl p-6 border border-[#22D3EE33]">
            <h3 class="font-semibold text-[#22D3EE] mb-2">The "Catalyst" tag</h3>
            <p class="text-[#CBD5E1] text-sm">
              The tag fires on a simple, fixed rule: the company has beaten estimates
              <strong class="text-white">at least two quarters running</strong> AND has
              <strong class="text-white">net positive analyst upgrades</strong> in the last 180 days. It's the
              "both engines firing" case — earnings momentum and Wall Street conviction at once. It only fires on
              a small slice of the market.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <!-- What the backtest showed -->
      <div class="bg-[#080A0F] rounded-2xl p-8 mb-12">
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold mb-4">What the backtest showed</h2>
        <p class="text-[#CBD5E1] mb-6">
          We tested 2016–2025 (the window where our delisted-company data is clean, so failed stocks aren't quietly
          dropped). The key discipline: every result is measured against a <strong class="text-white">matched
          control</strong> — peers with similar volatility and size — so we're not just rediscovering that risky
          stocks swing more.
        </p>
        <ul class="space-y-3 text-[#CBD5E1]">
          <li class="flex gap-3">
            <span class="text-emerald-400 font-bold">+</span>
            <span>In the 2016–2025 test, stocks with a strong catalyst signature hit a <strong class="text-white">+30%
            one-year gain about 1.5–3 percentage points more often</strong> than matched peers.</span>
          </li>
          <li class="flex gap-3">
            <span class="text-emerald-400 font-bold">+</span>
            <span>That edge held up even after also matching on price momentum and on value/quality — so it isn't
            just momentum or a cheap-stock effect wearing a costume.</span>
          </li>
          <li class="flex gap-3">
            <span class="text-emerald-400 font-bold">+</span>
            <span>It <strong class="text-white">lowered</strong> the rate of big drops, not raised it, and it
            pointed the right way in 8–9 of the 9 years tested.</span>
          </li>
          <li class="flex gap-3">
            <span class="text-amber-400 font-bold">!</span>
            <span>By contrast, the "fundamental trend" ideas we also tried — a company's margins or debt load
            improving, growth speeding up — <strong class="text-white">did not</strong> beat matched peers. The signal that
            worked was specifically about <em>expectations</em> — beats and upgrades.</span>
          </li>
        </ul>
      </div>

      <!-- The honest caveats -->
      <div class="bg-[#161B24] rounded-2xl p-8 mb-12 border border-amber-500/30">
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold mb-4 text-amber-300">The caveats, plainly</h2>
        <ul class="space-y-3 text-[#CBD5E1]">
          <li class="flex gap-3"><span class="text-amber-400">•</span>
            <span><strong class="text-white">Nine years is thin.</strong> The effect is real in the data but the
            sample is small, so the precise size could be smaller (or larger) than measured. We won't oversell it.</span></li>
          <li class="flex gap-3"><span class="text-amber-400">•</span>
            <span><strong class="text-white">It needs coverage.</strong> The signal only exists for stocks that
            report earnings and have analysts watching. Plenty of names show "No coverage," and that's honest.</span></li>
          <li class="flex gap-3"><span class="text-amber-400">•</span>
            <span><strong class="text-white">It's a probability nudge, not a promise.</strong> A higher chance of
            a big move is still just a chance. Most tagged stocks won't run; the signal is about the odds across
            many names, not a guarantee on any one.</span></li>
          <li class="flex gap-3"><span class="text-amber-400">•</span>
            <span><strong class="text-white">Forward-tracking is the real test.</strong> The formula is frozen.
            We'll let the live results speak, and we'll report them straight — including if they disappoint.</span></li>
        </ul>
      </div>

      <div class="text-center">
        <a href="/" class="inline-block bg-[#22D3EE] text-[#0C0F14] font-semibold px-6 py-3 rounded-lg hover:bg-[#1bb8d4] transition">
          Look up a stock's Catalyst Signal
        </a>
        <p class="text-xs text-[#64748B] mt-6 max-w-2xl mx-auto">
          Stockbrowse is for research and education, not financial advice. The Catalyst Signal is an experimental
          indicator based on past patterns; past performance doesn't guarantee future results. Do your own research
          or talk to a qualified advisor before investing.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</Layout>
